\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Proved the pure-marginal coupling identity and a positive quantum self-cost, excluding a zero-action constant-path formula for that diagnostic cost.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied definition, catalogue wording, source qualifications and reference entries for 30003674. The historical source/context and inherited status are retained; no fresh literature or novelty certification is claimed. No external theorem is used without proof in this attempt; explicit model assumptions are distinguished from facts about the source model.

\paragraph{A self-distance obstruction to a naive dynamic action.} The qualification in [S2] says the mean-field cost may have nonzero self-distance. Here is an exact finite-dimensional diagnostic. For a unit vector \(u\) let \(\rho=|u\rangle\langle u|\). Any positive bipartite density matrix \(\Gamma\) with both marginals \(\rho\) equals \(\rho\otimes\rho\). Indeed \(\operatorname{Tr}((I-\rho)\otimes I)\Gamma=0\); writing this as the squared Hilbert--Schmidt norm of \(((I-\rho)\otimes I)\Gamma^{1/2}\) proves support in \(\mathbb Cu\otimes\mathcal H\), and the other marginal reduces it to \(\mathbb Cu\otimes\mathbb Cu\).
For any self-adjoint matrix \(A\), the cost \(C=(A\otimes I-I\otimes A)^2\) therefore satisfies
\[
 \inf_{\Gamma\mapsto(\rho,\rho)}\operatorname{Tr}(C\Gamma)
 =2\big(\langle u,A^2u\rangle-\langle u,Au\rangle^2\big).
\]
Take \(A=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\), \(u=(1,1)/\sqrt2\): the answer is \(2\).
\paragraph{Failed proposed formula.} Any dynamic action which permits a constant state curve with zero velocity and assigns it zero nonnegative kinetic action has zero self-distance. It cannot equal the displayed static cost on all states. This does not preclude a quantum dynamic formula with an additional fluctuation term or different constraints. Nor is this finite matrix cost asserted to be exactly the report's unbounded position/momentum pseudo-metric. It isolates the conceptual obstacle that must be checked for that cost. Matching the actual mean-field quantity, admissible continuity equation and boundary terms remains the first gap; existing modern quantum metrics are not interchangeable with it.

